{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And you shall burn.</b> Rashi explains: this teaches that if the killer is found after the heifer's neck was broken, etc. And if you ask: but we say in the chapter Elu Na'erot — 'and the land shall not have atonement for the blood that is shed in it' — why do I need this to teach that if the killer is found after the heifer's neck was broken, they do not exempt him? And why do I need 'and you shall burn the innocent blood' to tell you that all who shed blood are juxtaposed to the heifer whose neck is broken, to say: just as the heifer whose neck is broken is from the neck, so too all who shed blood are from the neck? Rather, one may answer: the passage in Ketuvot follows the one who says strangulation is more severe, and had those who shed blood not been juxtaposed to the heifer whose neck is broken, to say that they are from the neck, I would have said a murderer is executed by strangulation because it is more severe — but it is not so. Rather, Rashi holds like the Rabbis, that the sword is more severe, and the law of executing a murderer is by the sword, by a fortiori from a slave: now, a slave who is killed — his execution is by the sword, which is more severe, as it is written 'נקום ינקם,' and 'vengeance' means nothing but the sword, as it is written 'חרב נוקמת נקם ברית' — all the more so one who kills a free person. 'And you shall burn' comes for what Rashi explained here, that if the killer is found after the neck was broken, etc. And if you ask: according to Rashi's explanation that 'and you shall burn' comes for if the killer is found after the neck was broken, then this verse 'and the land shall not have atonement for the blood that is shed in it' — how does he establish it? One may answer that he establishes it as taught in Karetot, that if the killer is found after the Day of Atonement, they execute him." "<b>[And you shall burn.</b> An expression of burning and consumption.]" "<b>When you do what is right.</b> If you do what is right and the commandment according to its law, you will burn away the punishment of the blood, for through the feeling of anxiety the matter will become known who struck him. <b>Completed, the section of the parashah Shofetim ve-Shotrim</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The references are to talmudic passages: Ketuvot 37b–38a (the chapter Elu Na'erot) and Karetot 11a. The argument turns on the talmudic debate over which form of execution is more severe (חמור), which determines the method of a murderer's execution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:41.922839411Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}